# Coding Conventions

## Simplicity First

- Keep the code as simple as possible. Prefer direct, readable implementations over clever abstractions.
- When investigating new directions, prefer creating separate classes, scripts, or experiment files instead of deeply integrating the idea into the main code path immediately.
- Many exploratory ideas will not be used in the bottom line, so keep experiments easy to remove or ignore.
- Add abstractions only after a pattern proves useful and repeated. In general, the simpler solution is better.

## PyTorch Patterns

- Use `torch.view_as_real` / `torch.view_as_complex` for complex↔real conversion, not manual splitting
- Register fixed tensors (e.g. channel realizations) as buffers: `self.register_buffer('H_d', ...)`
- Use `bmm` for batched matrix multiplication on GPU
- Power normalization: `s = s / s.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True) * sqrt(power)`

## Naming Conventions

| Symbol | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `n_t` / `Nt` | Number of transmit antennas |
| `n_r` / `Nr` | Number of receive antennas |
| `n_m` / `Nm` | Number of RIS elements |
| `s` | Transmit signal (complex) |
| `y` | Received signal (complex) |
| `H_d`, `H_1`, `H_2` | Channel matrices |
| `phi` / `theta` | RIS phase shifts |
| `B` | Batch size |

## Model Checkpointing

- Save checkpoints with meaningful names including phase and epoch
- Use `wandb` for all experiment logging — always log both task loss and auxiliary losses separately

## Training Functions

- All training logic lives in `training.py`
- Training functions follow the pattern: `train_<component>(model, dataloader, optimizer, ...)`
- Always support `num_channels_sample` parameter for channel sampling efficiency

## Error Checking

- Check for NaN in channel matching loss (division by zero in normalization)
- Verify `|phi_i| = 1` (unit modulus constraint) after phase optimization
- Verify power constraint: `||s||² ≈ power` after encoder

## HPC / Cluster

- This project runs on BGU SLURM cluster
- See `.cursor/docs/references/BGU_HPC_Cluster_User_Guide_Summary.md` for job submission details
- Use `wandb` offline mode when running on cluster nodes without internet

## Add Alongside, Don't Replace

- When introducing a new method, approach, or configuration, prefer to add it alongside the existing one rather than replacing it.
- Keep previously working options available — typically behind a flag or parameter — so we can switch back and directly compare old vs. new.
- Example: in `framework/cifar_minimal_dnn.py` the data pipeline supports BOTH
  CIFAR-10 and MNIST via `--data`, instead of replacing one with the other.
